# WBR Phase-1 engineering rules

The Mac checkout at `~/Desktop/Research/wbr-perception-phase1-runtime` is
canonical. The Raspberry Pi is a runtime and hardware-validation target.
Preserve existing work on both machines; deploy checksummed copies of Mac
utilities into a separate runtime bundle rather than resetting either checkout.

Before edits inspect Git status, current branch, recent history and relevant
files. Use feature branches; do not commit or push without explicit permission.
Major architecture, public API, node graph, message, build, dataset, experiment
format or report-structure changes require approval. Keep first-party experiment
utilities modular and preserve historical evidence unchanged. Document meaningful
changes, reproduction commands, test results, limitations and provenance.

Phase A is camera-free preparation. No camera-dependent command, camera process,
ORB-SLAM3, firmware/calibration change, or sensor capture may run until the user
explicitly says `CAMERA AVAILABLE`. Offline/synthetic tests prove tooling only.
Use the N1 → N2 → N3 gates in the Phase A runbook. Never weaken USB >=5000M,
the exact raw profile, timing thresholds or the mandatory 300-second N2 soak.
SDK device serial, ASIC serial, firmware-update ID and USB descriptor serial
are separate fields. Preserve calibration warnings and raw logs.

Keep acquisition local to the Pi; SSH is control only. Do not modify persistent
network configuration without approval. MicroSD is for OS, tools and small logs;
large datasets/bags require separately qualified storage. No broad device/sysfs
permissions. STM32H7 owns hard-real-time control and motor/jump safety.

Use English for code/comments/docs and the prompt language for explanations.
Test first-party behavior with offline/synthetic fixtures, label them explicitly,
and provide build/test/manual validation commands. Never expose credentials,
overwrite user work, modify upstream source to hide failures, or manufacture PASS.
# Claude Code Instructions — WBR Perception Runtime

Always reply to the user in English, even when the user writes in Vietnamese, unless they explicitly request Vietnamese.

## Read first

Before making architectural or implementation decisions, inspect:

- `AGENTS.md`
- `README.md`
- `docs/PROJECT_STATE.md`
- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`
- `docs/DECISIONS.md`
- `docs/NEXT_GATE.md`
- `docs/EVIDENCE_INDEX.md`

If one of these files does not yet exist or is clearly stale, report that rather than inventing its contents.

## Project

This repository is the Phase-1 perception/runtime research project for a wheel-biped robot.

Primary stack:

- Raspberry Pi 5;
- Ubuntu/Linux;
- ROS 2 Jazzy;
- Intel RealSense D435i;
- ORB-SLAM3.

The Pi owns perception, visual-inertial odometry, SLAM and non-hard-real-time estimation.
The STM32 owns hard-real-time balancing, control and actuator safety.

## Engineering rules

- Do not declare PASS without evidence.
- Do not modify hardware/system state unless the current ticket explicitly permits it.
- Preserve timestamp, frame and calibration provenance.
- Never confuse the D435i camera IMU with the robot body IMU.
- Do not jump to ORB-SLAM3 while an earlier sensor/ROS gate is unresolved.
- Compilation is not experimental validation.
- Prefer small, reversible changes.
- Run relevant tests before reporting completion.
- Never use destructive Git commands unless explicitly approved.
- Do not rewrite large parts of the repository merely to make them easier for the agent to understand.

## Hardware rule

One agent owns a stateful hardware execution session at a time.

Do not simultaneously mutate:

- Raspberry Pi system state;
- D435i runtime state;
- STM32 firmware state;
- a shared experiment directory.

## Return format

For execution tasks, return:

GOAL

STATUS
PASS / OPEN / BLOCKED

FILES INSPECTED

FILES CHANGED

COMMANDS RUN

TESTS

EVIDENCE

ASSUMPTIONS

RISKS

WHAT WAS NOT VERIFIED

NEXT RECOMMENDED ACTION
# WBR Perception Phase 1 Runtime

Runtime and experiment workspace for the wheel-biped robot perception project.

Current Phase-1 goal:

1. understand and benchmark official ORB-SLAM3;
2. validate Intel RealSense D435i sensing;
3. record repeatable ROS 2 sensor datasets;
4. validate ORB_SLAM3_ROS2 offline and live;
5. characterize failure modes before WBR integration.

Architecture:

D435i
→ ORB-SLAM3 / VIO
→ ROS 2
→ state estimation / autonomy
→ high-level references
→ STM32H7

STM32H7 retains ownership of hard-real-time balance, LQR, VMC, PID/PD,
jump control, actuator torque, CAN, and safety.

