The `.deb` itself is 8,905,600 bytes, SHA-256
`247890a83d7e9424cb3b476e22f0ec495c292453ea7338544397bf03d6006660`.
It contains `librealsense2.so.2.58.4`, `librealsense2-gl.so.2.58.4`, static
support libraries, headers, CMake/pkg-config exports, and tools including
`rs-enumerate-devices`, `rs-data-collect`, `rs-infrared`, `rs-motion` and
`rs-sensor-control`. There are **no udev rules or install scripts providing
device permissions** in the inspected archive. GUI/example tools are not a
reason to run a viewer or extra streams.

The native Linux backend expects V4L2 access plus IIO character devices and
write access to scan enables, buffer enable/length, sampling frequency,
hysteresis and trigger controls. HID access alone does not cover these
[SDK v2.58.4 operations](https://github.com/realsenseai/librealsense/blob/v2.58.4/src/linux/backend-hid.cpp).
This is the expected backend path; actual non-root streaming must confirm it.

Prepared `99-wbr-d435i.rules` matches **only serial 251843061466** and grants
`root:video 0660` access. A small root-owned udev helper grants the same access
only to the listed IIO control files after independently checking USB identity.
It does not write sensor settings or recursively chmod sysfs. No global 777,
passwordless sudo, new group or logout is required. Existing old login shells
without video membership would need logout/login. udev rules must be reloaded
and applied to the camera; subsequent reattachments use them automatically.

The user executes authenticated commands manually. SDK installation and udev
activation remain pending until normal-user verification confirms them.
Rollback: remove only the two newly installed WBR rule/helper files, reload
udev and physically reconnect this camera. Review apt history before removing
